PART 3: CREATING YOUR FIRST COMPONENT

================================================================================

Step 1: Create the Component File
Project Structure

Before:

src/
├── App.jsx
├── main.jsx
└── App.css


Create a components folder and Welcome.jsx:

src/
├── App.jsx
├── main.jsx
├── App.css
│
└── components/
    └── Welcome.jsx

Steps in VS Code
Step | Action
-----|--------------------------------------------
1    | Open the project in VS Code
2    | Right-click the src folder
3    | Select "New Folder"
4    | Name it components
5    | Right-click the components folder
6    | Select "New File"
7    | Name the file Welcome.jsx


Result:

src/components/Welcome.jsx


================================================================================

Step 2: Write Your First Component

================================================================================

File:

src/components/Welcome.jsx


Code:

export default function Welcome() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome to React!</h1>
      <p>This is my first component.</p>
    </div>
  );
}

Code Breakdown
Code / Concept                    | Explanation
----------------------------------|------------------------------------------
function Welcome()                | Creates component named Welcome
export default                    | Makes it available for default import
return (...)                      | Returns/describes the component UI
JSX                               | HTML-like syntax used inside React
<div>                             | Container for component content
<h1>                              | Displays heading
<p>                               | Displays paragraph

Easy Flow
Welcome()
    ↓
return (...)
    ↓
JSX
    ↓
React Renders Component
    ↓
Browser Shows Content


================================================================================

Understanding export default

================================================================================

export default function Welcome() {
  ...
}


export default makes Welcome the default export of the file.

This allows another file to import it:

import Welcome from './components/Welcome';


Flow:

Welcome.jsx
     ↓
export default Welcome
     ↓
App.jsx imports Welcome
     ↓
<Welcome />
     ↓
Component Rendered


================================================================================

Step 3: Import Component in App.jsx

================================================================================

Before - Without Welcome Component
import './App.css';

export default function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>My App</h1>
    </div>
  );
}

After - With Welcome Component
import './App.css';
import Welcome from './components/Welcome';

export default function App() {
  return (
    <div>

      <h1>My App</h1>

      <Welcome />

    </div>
  );
}

Important Changes
Code                                  | Purpose
--------------------------------------|--------------------------------------
import Welcome from './components/Welcome' | Imports Welcome component
./components/Welcome                  | Relative path to component file
<Welcome />                           | Uses/renders Welcome component


Depending on the project tooling, the .jsx extension can commonly be omitted in this import.

Important Note

The original example included:

import { useState } from 'react';


But useState was not actually used in this particular App component.

Therefore, it can be removed until state is required.

================================================================================

Understanding <Welcome />

================================================================================

<Welcome />


This tells React to use the Welcome component at that location.

App Component
     ↓
<h1>My App</h1>
     ↓
<Welcome />
     ↓
Welcome Component Executes
     ↓
Returns JSX
     ↓
Welcome Content Appears


<Welcome /> is self-closing because no child content is being placed between opening and closing tags.

Equivalent syntax:

<Welcome></Welcome>


But the shorter form is:

<Welcome />


